2. Memory-state model
Each memory should have several independent states.
Confidence state
Plain Text
confirmed
supported
tentative
conflicted
stale
unknown
Definitions
confirmed
Explicitly stated, entered, uploaded or later confirmed by the person.
supported
Strongly supported by repeated or corroborating context, but not explicitly confirmed as a permanent fact.
tentative
Possibly correct, but ambiguous or based partly on inference.
conflicted
Reliable records disagree.
stale
Previously reliable, but the fact may have changed and requires reconfirmation.
unknown
Insufficient evidence exists.
Consent state
Plain Text
session_only
remember
restricted
vault
pending_confirmation
forget_requested
deleted
session_only
 May be used during the current session, but must not enter durable memory.
remember
 May be stored and used for appropriate continuity.
restricted
 May be stored, but use is controlled by explicit permissions.
vault
 Stored in the protected vault and unavailable to ordinary recall.
pending_confirmation
 Potentially useful memory awaiting user approval.
forget_requested
 Immediately excluded from retrieval while deletion is processed.
deleted
 Content has been deleted or cryptographically erased, with only a minimal non-content deletion audit record retained if required.
Sensitivity state
Plain Text
ordinary
personal
sensitive
highly_sensitive
secret_prohibited
secret_prohibited is for data that IZA should refuse to store as normal memory, including raw passwords, banking PINs, recovery codes or private cryptographic keys.
Temporal state
Plain Text
current
historical
time_limited
needs_reconfirmation
superseded
This stops an old fact from becoming a permanent assumption.
Lifecycle state
Plain Text
candidate
active
restricted
suppressed
scheduled_for_deletion
