temporal_state: time_limited
valid_until: trip end date, if known
If the end date is unknown, ask for it or attach expiry to the trip event's completion state.
At expiry, either delete the memory or request whether it should be retained, depending on the original instruction and product setting.

Edge case 18: Forget one fact, not the whole person
Person
“Forget Sarah's address.”
Result
Delete only the address memory and any derived address embedding or link.
Do not delete:
Sarah's identity
unrelated shared memories
project relationships
all previous conversations mentioning Sarah
Deletion scope must match the request.

Edge case 19: “Forget Sarah”
This is broader but still needs precise execution.
IZA should identify what will be affected:
“That would remove the memories and relationship links centred on Sarah. It won't rewrite old documents or messages unless you choose those too.”
For destructive bulk deletion, require clear confirmation and show the scope before execution.

Edge case 20: Information appears inside an uploaded document
Uploading a document grants permission to process it for the requested task. It does not automatically grant permission to convert every statement in it into permanent personal memory.
Plain Text
document processing consent != permanent memory consent
Document chunks may remain in the document knowledge index according to the upload/storage settings, while personal memory extraction follows separate consent rules.

13. Required tests
The following should become automated policy tests.
Plain Text
TEST-MEM-001
A vault memory never appears in vector search.
 
TEST-MEM-002
A forget-requested memory becomes unavailable immediately.
 
TEST-MEM-003
A high-confidence restricted memory cannot be surfaced proactively.
 
TEST-MEM-004
A tentative memory is never phrased as confirmed fact.
 
TEST-MEM-005
A stale current-state memory triggers reconfirmation language.
 
TEST-MEM-006
A voice-blocked memory cannot enter a voice response.
 
TEST-MEM-007
A memory blocked from an agent cannot enter that agent's prompt.
 
TEST-MEM-008
A third-party sensitive memory cannot create unrestricted profiling.
 
TEST-MEM-009
A user correction supersedes active use of the incorrect fact.
 
TEST-MEM-010
A prohibited secret cannot be persisted or embedded.
 
TEST-MEM-011
Expired consent blocks further retrieval.
 
TEST-MEM-012
A session-only memory is unavailable in a later session.
 
TEST-MEM-013
Deleting one fact does not delete unrelated memories.
 
TEST-MEM-014
Every persistent memory has provenance.
 
TEST-MEM-015
Every memory query is scoped to the authenticated user_id.
14. Final implementation rule
Plain Text
IZA MUST NEVER decide that a memory is safe to disclose merely
because the memory was successfully retrieved.
 
The enforced order is:
 
RETRIEVE
→ VERIFY CONFIDENCE
→ CHECK TEMPORAL STATE
→ CHECK CONSENT
→ CHECK SENSITIVITY
→ CHECK CHANNEL
→ CHECK AUDIENCE/CONTEXT
→ AUTHORISE MINIMUM NECESSARY USE
→ BUILD PROMPT
→ SPEAK NATURALLY
→ RECORD THE USE EVENT
The central principle is:
Memory tells IZA what may be known. Consent determines what may be done with it. Context determines whether now is the right time.
This needs to become an enforced subsystem, not just instructions inside IZA’s character prompt. The language model can interpret what the person says, but the backend must control what is stored, retrieved, surfaced, shared and deleted.
The design below keeps confidence, consent, sensitivity, time and provenance separate. That follows the privacy principle of only processing information needed for an authorised purpose, with sufficiently granular controls over data use. [pages.nist.gov], [nist.gov]
IZA Memory-State Implementation Requirements
1. Core memory laws
Plain Text
MEM-CORE-001
 
Every persistent memory MUST belong to exactly one authenticated user_id.
All reads, writes, links, retrievals and deletions MUST enforce
user_id isolation through PostgreSQL Row-Level Security.
Plain Text
MEM-CORE-002
 
The system MUST represent these independently:
 
1. Content
2. Confidence
3. Consent
4. Sensitivity
5. Temporal state
6. Provenance
7. Lifecycle state
8. Permitted uses
 
No single score may replace these states.
Plain Text
MEM-CORE-003
 
High confidence MUST NOT override restrictive consent.
 
High relevance MUST NOT override sensitivity controls.
 
High salience MUST NOT override a do-not-surface restriction.
 
A successful vector match MUST NOT be treated as proof that
the retrieved information is accurate.
Plain Text
MEM-CORE-004
 
Memory retrieval and memory disclosure MUST be separate operations.
 
Retrieval means:
"This information may be relevant internally."
 
Disclosure means:
"IZA has permission to use this information in the current response."
 
A retrieved memory MUST pass the consent policy engine before being
placed into an AI prompt or spoken to the person.
Plain Text
MEM-CORE-005
 
Information stored in the Continuity Vault MUST remain outside:
 
- vector embeddings
- standard semantic search
- ordinary conversational recall
- AI prompts
- agent hand-offs
- proactive notifications
- analytics content
 
Vault information may only be accessed through the dedicated
authorised vault workflow.

2. Memory-state model
Each memory should have several independent states.
Confidence state
Plain Text
confirmed
supported
tentative
conflicted
stale
unknown
Definitions
confirmed
Explicitly stated, entered, uploaded or later confirmed by the person.
supported
Strongly supported by repeated or corroborating context, but not explicitly confirmed as a permanent fact.
tentative
Possibly correct, but ambiguous or based partly on inference.
conflicted
Reliable records disagree.
stale
Previously reliable, but the fact may have changed and requires reconfirmation.
unknown
Insufficient evidence exists.
Consent state
Plain Text
session_only
remember
restricted
vault
pending_confirmation
forget_requested
deleted
session_only
 May be used during the current session, but must not enter durable memory.
remember
 May be stored and used for appropriate continuity.
restricted
 May be stored, but use is controlled by explicit permissions.
vault
 Stored in the protected vault and unavailable to ordinary recall.
pending_confirmation
 Potentially useful memory awaiting user approval.
forget_requested
 Immediately excluded from retrieval while deletion is processed.
deleted
 Content has been deleted or cryptographically erased, with only a minimal non-content deletion audit record retained if required.
Sensitivity state
Plain Text
ordinary
personal
sensitive
highly_sensitive
secret_prohibited
secret_prohibited is for data that IZA should refuse to store as normal memory, including raw passwords, banking PINs, recovery codes or private cryptographic keys.
Temporal state
Plain Text
current
historical
time_limited
needs_reconfirmation
superseded
This stops an old fact from becoming a permanent assumption.
Lifecycle state
Plain Text
candidate
active
restricted
suppressed
scheduled_for_deletion
deleted

