9. Memory ingestion requirements
Explicit memory requests
Plain Text
MEM-INGEST-001
 
When the person explicitly says "remember this", IZA SHOULD create
a candidate persistent memory.
 
Before activation, the system MUST:
 
- classify sensitivity
- identify the subject
- record provenance
- identify the requested scope
- reject prohibited secrets
- detect conflicts
- assign consent permissions
Incidental conversation
Plain Text
MEM-INGEST-002
 
Ordinary conversation MUST NOT automatically become permanent memory
solely because it was spoken.
 
The system MAY create a candidate when information has legitimate
continuity value, but sensitive or ambiguous candidates MUST require
confirmation before durable activation.
Prohibited secrets
Plain Text
MEM-INGEST-003
 
Passwords, banking PINs, recovery codes, private cryptographic keys
and equivalent authentication secrets MUST NOT be stored as ordinary
memories or included in embeddings.
 
IZA should interrupt the storage flow and direct the person to the
protected vault or an appropriate password manager workflow.
Inferred memories
Plain Text
MEM-INGEST-004
 
An agent inference MUST be labelled as inferred.
 
An inferred memory MUST NOT receive confirmed status unless the
person explicitly confirms it.
 
Sensitive inferred attributes MUST NOT be stored automatically.

10. Recall and disclosure decision
Use two functions:
Plain Text
recall_candidates()
finds potentially relevant memories.
Plain Text
authorise_memory_use()
decides whether each result may influence the response.
Decision structure
TypeScript
type MemoryUseContext = {
userId: string;
agentId: string;
channel: "text" | "voice" | "notification" | "export";
purpose:
| "direct_answer"
| "proactive_follow_up"
| "recommendation"
| "agent_handoff"
| "private_export";
privateContextConfirmed: boolean;
freshAuthentication: boolean;
directlyRequestedByUser: boolean;
};
TypeScript
type MemoryDecision = {
allowed: boolean;
mode:
| "use_naturally"
| "ask_confirmation"
| "use_with_uncertainty"
| "historical_only"
| "do_not_surface";
reasonCode: string;
};
Decision logic
TypeScript
function authoriseMemoryUse(
memory: Memory,
policy: MemoryConsentPolicy,
context: MemoryUseContext
): MemoryDecision {
if (
memory.lifecycleState === "deleted" ||
memory.lifecycleState === "scheduled_for_deletion" ||
memory.consentState === "deleted" ||
memory.consentState === "forget_requested"
) {
return {
allowed: false,
mode: "do_not_surface",
reasonCode: "MEMORY_REMOVED"
};
}
 
if (memory.sensitivityState === "secret_prohibited") {
return {
allowed: false,
mode: "do_not_surface",
reasonCode: "PROHIBITED_SECRET"
};
}
 
if (memory.consentState === "session_only" && !memory.isCurrentSession) {
return {
allowed: false,
mode: "do_not_surface",
reasonCode: "SESSION_EXPIRED"
};
}
 
if (memory.consentState === "vault") {
return {
allowed: false,
mode: "do_not_surface",
reasonCode: "VAULT_ISOLATION"
};
}
 
if (
context.channel === "voice" &&
!policy.allowVoiceSurface
) {
return {
allowed: false,
mode: "do_not_surface",
reasonCode: "VOICE_NOT_ALLOWED"
};
}
 
if (
context.purpose === "proactive_follow_up" &&
!policy.allowProactiveSurface
) {
return {
allowed: false,
mode: "do_not_surface",
reasonCode: "PROACTIVE_USE_NOT_ALLOWED"
};
}
 
if (
policy.requiresPrivateContext &&
!context.privateContextConfirmed
) {
return {
allowed: false,
mode: "ask_confirmation",
reasonCode: "PRIVATE_CONTEXT_REQUIRED"
};
}
 
if (
policy.requiresFreshAuth &&
!context.freshAuthentication
) {
return {
allowed: false,
mode: "ask_confirmation",
reasonCode: "FRESH_AUTH_REQUIRED"
};
}
 
if (
memory.confidenceState === "tentative" ||
memory.confidenceState === "unknown"
) {
return {
allowed: true,
mode: "use_with_uncertainty",
reasonCode: "LOW_CONFIDENCE"
};
}
 
if (
memory.confidenceState === "conflicted"
) {
return {
allowed: false,
mode: "ask_confirmation",
reasonCode: "CONFLICT_REQUIRES_RESOLUTION"
};
}
 
if (
memory.confidenceState === "stale" ||
memory.temporalState === "needs_reconfirmation"
) {
return {
allowed: true,
mode: "historical_only",
reasonCode: "CURRENT_STATUS_UNCONFIRMED"
};
}
 
return {
allowed: true,
mode: "use_naturally",
reasonCode: "AUTHORISED"
};
}
This function should run before prompt construction, not after the model has already seen the memory.

11. Prompt-construction rule
Plain Text
MEM-PROMPT-001
 
Only memories with an authorised decision may enter an AI prompt.
 
The prompt builder MUST include:
 
- authorised memory content
- confidence instruction
- temporal instruction
- disclosure mode
 
The prompt builder MUST exclude:
 
- restricted unauthorised memories
- deleted memories
- vault memories
- prohibited secrets
- unrelated memories
- raw internal scores unless operationally necessary
Example internal prompt fragment:
Plain Text
Authorised continuity context:
 
Memory:
Sarah is recorded as the person's business partner.
 
Confidence:
Confirmed.
 
Temporal status:
Current as of the latest confirmation.
 
Permitted use:
May be used naturally in direct conversation.
Do not mention database storage, confidence scores or retrieval.

12. Consent edge cases
Edge case 1: “Remember this”
Person
“Remember that Sarah is my business partner.”
Result
Plain Text
consent_state: remember
confidence_state: confirmed
sensitivity_state: personal
temporal_state: current
allow_conversational_recall: true
allow_proactive_surface: default user setting
Natural future use
“Has Sarah seen the new version?”

Edge case 2: “Don't remember this”
Person
“Don't remember this, but I'm considering leaving my job.”
Result
Plain Text
consent_state: session_only
persistent memory: prohibited
session use: allowed
post-session retention: no
IZA can discuss it now but cannot intentionally make it a durable memory.

Edge case 3: “Don't bring it up unless I ask”
Person
“You can remember the diagnosis, but don't bring it up unless I ask.”
Result
Plain Text
consent_state: restricted
sensitivity_state: highly_sensitive
allow_conversational_recall: true
allow_proactive_surface: false
allow_voice_surface: false by default
requires_private_context: true
This means retain but do not proactively mention. It does not mean delete.

Edge case 4: “Forget that”
Person
“Forget what I said about leaving my job.”
Result
Plain Text
consent_state: forget_requested
lifecycle_state: scheduled_for_deletion
immediate retrieval exclusion: required
embedding removal: required
links review/removal: required
attachment review: required
After deletion:
Plain Text
consent_state: deleted
lifecycle_state: deleted
content: removed
embedding: removed
IZA should not later say:
“I remember something you asked me to forget.”

Edge case 5: Ambiguous “keep that”
Person
“Keep that.”
Context might mean save the document, retain the current design, or remember a personal fact.
Result
Plain Text
consent_state: pending_confirmation
Natural clarification
“Keep the file, or remember what you just told me?”
Do not interpret ambiguous language as broad consent.

Edge case 6: Group instruction
Person
“Remember everything about the IZA project.”
This is broad consent, but not permission to capture unrelated secrets or third-party sensitive information.
Result
Plain Text
scope: project = IZA
allow persistent project memory: true
sensitivity rules: still enforced
secret prohibition: still enforced
third-party restrictions: still enforced
Consent scope must not silently expand beyond the defined project.

Edge case 7: Password supplied with “remember it”
Person
“Remember my banking password. It's…”
Result
Plain Text
sensitivity_state: secret_prohibited
persistent ordinary memory: blocked
embedding: prohibited
prompt retention: minimised
IZA
“I won't put a banking password into ordinary memory. Store it in a proper password manager or protected vault instead.”
The instruction to remember does not override the prohibited-secret rule.

Edge case 8: Health information in public voice context
IZA knows a confirmed health detail, but the person opens voice mode while other people may be nearby.
Result
Plain Text
allow_voice_surface: false
requires_private_context: true
IZA
“I've found the relevant information. Want me to show it privately instead?”
Do not read the sensitive detail aloud.

Edge case 9: Old relationship status
Historical memory:
“Sarah is my partner.”
Newer memory:
“Sarah and I separated.”
Result
Plain Text
old memory:
temporal_state: historical or superseded
 
new memory:
confidence_state: confirmed
temporal_state: current
IZA must not continue calling Sarah the person's current partner.

Edge case 10: Contradictory information
Older:
“My appointment is Tuesday.”
Newer:
“They moved it to Thursday.”
Result
The newer explicit correction supersedes the earlier schedule. The old information may be retained as historical provenance but excluded from current answers.
IZA
“It's Thursday now.”
Not:
“I have conflicting memories for Tuesday and Thursday.”

Edge case 11: Inferred family relationship
The person frequently discusses Sarah and their children, but never states Sarah's relationship.
Result
Plain Text
confidence_state: tentative
origin_type: agent_inference
relationship label: not confirmed
IZA
“Is Sarah your partner?”
Not:
“How is your partner Sarah?”

Edge case 12: Third-party health information
Person
“My friend Daniel has cancer.”
Result
Plain Text
subject_type: third_party
sensitivity_state: highly_sensitive
allow_relationship_linking: minimal
allow_external_processing: false by default
allow_proactive_surface: false by default
Store only what is necessary for the user's continuity. Do not create an unrestricted medical profile for Daniel.

Edge case 13: “This stays between us”
This indicates confidentiality but is ambiguous about persistence.
Preferred handling
“I can keep it restricted. Do you want me to remember it privately, or only use it in this conversation?”
Until confirmed:
Plain Text
consent_state: session_only
sensitivity_state: sensitive

Edge case 14: “Don't tell the Doctor Agent”
Result
Plain Text
consent_state: restricted
blocked_agent_ids: ['iza_doctor']
allow_agent_access: true for other specifically authorised agents
A memory can be available to IZA while blocked from one specialist agent.

Edge case 15: “Keep it out of work mode”
Result
Plain Text
consent_state: restricted
permitted_domains: ['personal']
The memory must not be included in work-agent prompts, workplace summaries or work-related exports.

Edge case 16: User changes their mind
First:
“Don't store this.”
Later:
“Actually, remember it.”
Result
Create an explicit consent-expansion event. Do not pretend the later permission existed during the earlier restricted period.

Edge case 17: Temporary consent
Person
“Remember this until the trip is over.”
Result
Plain Text
consent_state: remember
temporal_state: time_limited
valid_until: trip end date, if known
If the end date is unknown, ask for it or attach expiry to the trip event's completion state.
At expiry, either delete the memory or request whether it should be retained, depending on the original instruction and product setting.

Edge case 18: Forget one fact, not the whole person
Person
“Forget Sarah's address.”
Result
Delete only the address memory and any derived address embedding or link.
Do not delete:
Sarah's identity
unrelated shared memories
project relationships
all previous conversations mentioning Sarah
Deletion scope must match the request.

Edge case 19: “Forget Sarah”
This is broader but still needs precise execution.
IZA should identify what will be affected:
“That would remove the memories and relationship links centred on Sarah. It won't rewrite old documents or messages unless you choose those too.”
